{
 "answer": {
  "answer": "The study did not meet its goal. It confirmed 3 of the 5 cases it was asked to find, each on a different scene: 0e002edd at 1.4x / \u22121.5 s (S19), 07981e6a at 0.6x / +1.5 s (S3) and 1bbe02fc at 0.8x / \u22122.0 s (S31). Each of these is backed by only three repeats, so the true failure rate could be anywhere from just above one half to always. None of the failures involved the pedestrian. The triage shows every one was the policy hitting a parked car, rear-ending or sideswiping another vehicle, or leaving the road, almost always after drifting left or ignoring a RIGHT route command. So the study shows where VaVAM at 2 Hz fails in these scenes, but not that the pedestrian's step-out time causes it. Ego speed seems to matter on two scenes (0e002edd and 18d28973 failed only at 1.4x), but that rests on single runs at the lower speeds. Two caveats: two of S19's failures had the ego far off the recorded path, so the simulator may partly explain them; and two of S31's three failures were marginal off-road flags at the curb edge.",
  "findings": [
   {
    "claim": "No failed run hit the retimed pedestrian. Every failure was a collision with a parked car or another vehicle, or the ego leaving the road. The study therefore did not show any ego-speed and step-out-time combination that leads to a pedestrian strike.",
    "settings": [
     "S1",
     "S2",
     "S3",
     "S4",
     "S5",
     "S13",
     "S15",
     "S19",
     "S29",
     "S30",
     "S31",
     "S32",
     "S33"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "Confirmed case on 0e002edd at 1.4x / \u22121.5 s: every run failed. The ego cut the right turn too tight and hit parked car 90, or crept left into oncoming car 112. At the same \u22121.5 s shift, the single runs at 0.8x and 1.0x did not fail, which hints that high hand-off speed drives this. Two of the failures were flagged as possible simulator artifacts (ego well off the recorded path), although triage put them on the policy.",
    "settings": [
     "S19",
     "S16",
     "S17",
     "S18"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "Confirmed case on 07981e6a at 0.6x / +1.5 s: every run failed. The scene failed at every setting tried, from 0.6x to 1.2x and shifts from \u22121.0 s to +1.5 s, and always the same way: the ego ignored the right turn, drifted left into the parking lane and hit parked cars without braking. This scene fails regardless of speed or timing and fills a slot cheaply. S4's single failure may be a simulator artifact.",
    "settings": [
     "S3",
     "S2",
     "S4",
     "S5"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "Confirmed case on 1bbe02fc at 0.8x / \u22122.0 s: every run failed, and the single runs at 1.0x / \u22121.5 s and 1.4x / \u22121.5 s failed too. The policy planned straight or left instead of the right turn. However, two of S31's failures are off-road flags with the ego under 1 m from the human path (one rated 'unclear' in triage), so this case may partly reflect map-edge flagging. S33 may be a simulator artifact (smeared camera, ego far off the path).",
    "settings": [
     "S31",
     "S32",
     "S33"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "18d28973 failed only at 1.4x, at both \u22121.0 s and +1.0 s, by rear-ending the slower sedan ahead (actor 99) while barely braking. The single runs at 0.8x and 1.2x did not fail. Speed, not pedestrian timing, looks like the factor, but neither 1.4x setting is confirmed yet.",
    "settings": [
     "S29",
     "S30",
     "S25",
     "S26",
     "S27",
     "S28"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "Two other scenes are close to confirming. On 0d4893f5 at 1.4x / +1.5 s, both runs failed when the ego drifted left into vehicle 33. On 048b974e at 1.2x / \u22121.0 s, most runs failed when the ego steered left into parked car 68. On 0d4893f5, 0.8x / +2.0 s failed only rarely.",
    "settings": [
     "S15",
     "S13",
     "S14",
     "S1"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "Three scenes produced no failures at any setting tried. On 12a09194 the ego never got close to any actor, on 0b10bce8 it stayed clear, and 0caa8f1a stayed clear even at 1.4x / \u22122.0 s.",
    "settings": [
     "S20",
